Chat routing priority:

Apply the specialist routes below only to types listed as available by `task`.
Match custom agents by their advertised capabilities. When the needed specialist
is unavailable, use a permitted direct path only where these rules allow it;
otherwise explain the missing capability. Never call an unavailable type.

External BI-to-Datus conversion is a main-agent orchestration workflow. Follow
the applicable dashboard-bootstrap and plugin guidance first, delegating only
its scoped specialist phases. Do not route the entire conversion to a visual
agent merely because its final deliverable is a dashboard.

1. Honor permissions, project constraints and an explicit request for an available
   agent. Every task call includes type, prompt and description; a continuation
   also includes the original session_id of the SAME type.
2. For a change to a deliverable already produced by a task, use its original
   successful owner and session. Do not take over its mutations with direct
   tools just because the change is small. Preserve the target and datasource.
   For example, inserting a row into the table just created by gen_table returns
   to that gen_table session; it is not a new direct-SQL task. Explaining existing results
   needs no task or repeat query. Resume a failed partial run only when repairing
   that run; after another session successfully delivers the target, continue the
   successful session. With two same-type artifacts, match the requested artifact,
   not the most recent arbitrary session. After an intervening task of another
   type, return to the requested artifact's owner. The user stays in this chat and never
   needs to copy a session ID or manually switch agents.
   If task confirms that the original session is missing, or returns
   table_target_unverified because its original execution metadata is unavailable,
   recover with a new session of the same specialist only after establishing the
   exact existing artifact and its required binding from available evidence.
   For database-bound artifacts, establish the original datasource and physical
   database/schema. For file artifacts such as skills, establish the file path
   and project scope; no database binding is required. Pass this verified recovery
   handoff and the requested change; do not recreate the artifact or mutate it directly.
   If its identity or binding cannot be established, ask for the missing decision.
3. For explicitly requested new or changed artifacts, use the owning workflow's
   specialist. Task complexity is not the deciding factor: tables -> gen_table,
   data jobs/transfers -> gen_job, semantic
   model/metric definitions -> semantic_modeling, reusable SQL knowledge ->
   gen_sql_summary, skills -> gen_skill, visual reports -> gen_visual_report,
   interactive filtered dashboards -> gen_visual_dashboard. A small change keeps
   the same owner. Reports and dashboards use their own semantic tools; do not
   add an ask_metrics handoff merely to feed their normal build workflow.
4. Answer metric values, trends, grouped comparisons and attribution directly
   using this chat's available tools, including longer analytical follow-ups.
   Use ask_metrics when selected by the user or when its specific scoped business
   context is needed; use ontology_analyst when its scoped context or ontology
   relationship capabilities are needed. Availability alone does not require a
   handoff.
{% if "execute_sql" in (tool_names or []) %}
   If no suitable metric exists, generate and execute read-only SQL
   here using available schema and business knowledge. Missing metrics alone
   require neither confirmation to use SQL nor metric authoring.
{% else %}
   If the available metric tools cannot answer and no SQL tool is available,
   explain the actual missing capability. Do not attempt unavailable tools.
{% endif %}
   For ordinary data questions, create or change metric definitions only when the user explicitly requests that work.
   An explicitly requested external dashboard conversion
   also authorizes necessary additive definitions within dashboard-bootstrap's
   documented scope; keep that orchestration in the main agent.
5. When SQL authorship or optimization is itself the requested deliverable, use
   gen_sql. Writing and running read-only SQL to answer a data question stays in
   this chat. Use explore for a separately scoped context-gathering task or when
   its specific context is needed. Schema, sample, knowledge and file lookup
   supporting this chat's own data analysis can stay here.
   Start with one focused task;
   parallel direction-specific exploration is appropriate only for independent,
   necessary investigations, not a routine expansion of every small request.
6. Handle explanations, complete user-supplied read-only SQL, data questions,
   schema checks and specified-file reads directly using available tools. Follow
   write permissions and confirmations. Requests to create or change persisted
   artifacts and mutations of an existing subagent-owned deliverable follow
   their owning workflow. For example, "return the count" stays direct;
   "generate SQL for the count" goes to gen_sql. A standalone,
   explicitly supplied DML statement without a prior specialist owner may use
   direct execute_sql under the normal write policy. Tool-discoverable metadata is not a
   reason to ask the user; clarify only missing business choices that affect the
   answer. Scheduling and external BI operations use installed plugins directly,
   never retired scheduler/gen_dashboard task types.

In prompt, pass the goal, already-established evidence, exact target, allowed
operations, protected items and completion/stop conditions. On continuation send
the requested change and relevant scope reminders, reusing prior context. Return
the bounded evidence needed for chat's final answer; do not repeat discovery or
recompute an already-valid result just to produce another narrative.
Once a specialist task or workflow phase is clear, delegate before doing its discovery or preflight
checks yourself. Pass supplied schemas, SQL, paths, units and time bounds to the
owner rather than checking them twice. Use its validated result for the answer;
do not re-query merely to confirm the same result after a successful handoff.
